1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,081
Today your scripts learn to speak to the command line.

2
00:00:03,631 --> 00:00:04,735
Run by a person,

3
00:00:04,735 --> 00:00:06,667
a script can explain itself.

4
00:00:06,667 --> 00:00:07,908
Run by a pipeline,

5
00:00:07,908 --> 00:00:09,357
it only has a number.

6
00:00:09,357 --> 00:00:13,910
Three habits make a script a proper tool: arguments with argparse,

7
00:00:13,910 --> 00:00:14,669
exit codes,

8
00:00:14,669 --> 00:00:16,325
and two output channels.

9
00:00:16,875 --> 00:00:20,160
Every demo is typed live on Ubuntu twenty-four oh four.

10
00:00:22,210 --> 00:00:24,744
A script you run by hand can talk to you.

11
00:00:24,744 --> 00:00:27,217
A script that runs in a pipeline cannot.

12
00:00:27,217 --> 00:00:29,566
So it needs a clear way to take input,

13
00:00:29,566 --> 00:00:31,853
and a clear way to report the result.

14
00:00:32,403 --> 00:00:34,569
Input comes from the command line.

15
00:00:34,569 --> 00:00:36,736
Arguments are what the tool needs,

16
00:00:36,736 --> 00:00:37,883
flags are options.

17
00:00:37,883 --> 00:00:39,158
Argparse reads them,

18
00:00:39,158 --> 00:00:40,369
checks their types,

19
00:00:40,369 --> 00:00:42,472
and even writes the help for you.

20
00:00:43,022 --> 00:00:44,914
The result is the exit code.

21
00:00:44,914 --> 00:00:46,198
Zero means success.

22
00:00:46,198 --> 00:00:48,090
Anything else means failure.

23
00:00:48,090 --> 00:00:49,037
By convention,

24
00:00:49,037 --> 00:00:50,185
one is a failure,

25
00:00:50,185 --> 00:00:53,091
and two means the command was used wrongly.

26
00:00:53,641 --> 00:00:55,460
And output has two channels.

27
00:00:55,460 --> 00:00:57,343
Standard out carries results.

28
00:00:57,343 --> 00:00:59,421
Standard error carries problems.

29
00:00:59,421 --> 00:01:02,473
A pipeline can keep one and show you the other.

30
00:01:03,023 --> 00:01:07,546
The rule: arguments in, exit code out, and problems to standard error.

31
00:01:09,596 --> 00:01:11,206
Let's build a real tool,

32
00:01:11,206 --> 00:01:13,554
one you could drop into a pipeline.

33
00:01:13,554 --> 00:01:16,305
It checks whether a network port is open.

34
00:01:16,305 --> 00:01:17,378
Show the script.

35
00:01:17,378 --> 00:01:18,049
Type: cat,

36
00:01:18,049 --> 00:01:19,324
port check dot P Y.

37
00:01:20,174 --> 00:01:21,157
Sixteen lines.

38
00:01:21,157 --> 00:01:23,966
Argparse reads the command line: a host,

39
00:01:23,966 --> 00:01:26,003
a port that must be a number,

40
00:01:26,003 --> 00:01:27,969
and an optional dash Q flag.

41
00:01:27,969 --> 00:01:30,638
The try and except decides the result,

42
00:01:30,638 --> 00:01:34,571
and the number the script returns becomes the exit code.

43
00:01:34,571 --> 00:01:36,467
Ask the tool how to use it.

44
00:01:36,467 --> 00:01:37,591
Type: dot slash,

45
00:01:37,591 --> 00:01:38,925
port check dot P Y,

46
00:01:38,925 --> 00:01:39,417
dash H.

47
00:01:39,967 --> 00:01:41,349
Free documentation,

48
00:01:41,349 --> 00:01:43,969
written by argparse: the usage line,

49
00:01:43,969 --> 00:01:45,278
the two arguments,

50
00:01:45,278 --> 00:01:46,879
and the dash Q option.

51
00:01:46,879 --> 00:01:47,680
Now use it,

52
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:48,771
on the lab API.

53
00:01:48,771 --> 00:01:49,935
Type: dot slash,

54
00:01:49,935 --> 00:01:51,318
port check dot P Y,

55
00:01:51,318 --> 00:01:52,118
local host,

56
00:01:52,118 --> 00:01:53,428
eighty eighty-one.

57
00:01:53,978 --> 00:01:54,308
Open.

58
00:01:54,308 --> 00:01:55,432
The port answers,

59
00:01:55,432 --> 00:01:57,811
and the result went to standard out.

60
00:01:57,811 --> 00:02:00,982
But the script also left a number for the shell.

61
00:02:00,982 --> 00:02:01,709
Ask for it.

62
00:02:01,709 --> 00:02:02,436
Type: echo,

63
00:02:02,436 --> 00:02:03,229
dollar sign,

64
00:02:03,229 --> 00:02:04,154
question mark.

65
00:02:04,704 --> 00:02:05,082
Zero.

66
00:02:05,082 --> 00:02:06,519
Zero means success.

67
00:02:06,519 --> 00:02:09,392
Now a port where nothing is listening.

68
00:02:09,392 --> 00:02:10,602
Type: dot slash,

69
00:02:10,602 --> 00:02:12,039
port check dot P Y,

70
00:02:12,039 --> 00:02:12,871
local host,

71
00:02:12,871 --> 00:02:13,173
one.

72
00:02:13,723 --> 00:02:14,211
Closed,

73
00:02:14,211 --> 00:02:15,533
connection refused,

74
00:02:15,533 --> 00:02:16,716
and no traceback.

75
00:02:16,716 --> 00:02:19,153
The problem went to standard error.

76
00:02:19,153 --> 00:02:20,684
Ask for the exit code.

77
00:02:20,684 --> 00:02:21,450
Type: echo,

78
00:02:21,450 --> 00:02:22,285
dollar sign,

79
00:02:22,285 --> 00:02:23,259
question mark.

80
00:02:23,809 --> 00:02:24,098
One.

81
00:02:24,098 --> 00:02:24,674
Failure.

82
00:02:24,674 --> 00:02:26,116
Any caller can tell,

83
00:02:26,116 --> 00:02:28,278
without reading a single word.

84
00:02:28,278 --> 00:02:32,243
Now a mistake by the user: a port that is not a number.

85
00:02:32,243 --> 00:02:33,396
Type: dot slash,

86
00:02:33,396 --> 00:02:34,765
port check dot P Y,

87
00:02:34,765 --> 00:02:35,558
local host,

88
00:02:35,558 --> 00:02:35,991
A B C.

89
00:02:36,541 --> 00:02:37,927
Argparse refused it,

90
00:02:37,927 --> 00:02:39,175
printed the usage,

91
00:02:39,175 --> 00:02:40,770
and gave a clear error,

92
00:02:40,770 --> 00:02:41,741
all by itself,

93
00:02:41,741 --> 00:02:43,474
and the script never ran.

94
00:02:43,474 --> 00:02:44,514
What exit code?

95
00:02:44,514 --> 00:02:45,277
Type: echo,

96
00:02:45,277 --> 00:02:46,109
dollar sign,

97
00:02:46,109 --> 00:02:47,079
question mark.

98
00:02:47,629 --> 00:02:47,895
Two.

99
00:02:47,895 --> 00:02:49,952
By convention: zero is success,

100
00:02:49,952 --> 00:02:50,948
one is failure,

101
00:02:50,948 --> 00:02:53,802
and two means the command was used wrongly.

102
00:02:53,802 --> 00:02:54,797
Three outcomes,

103
00:02:54,797 --> 00:02:55,727
three numbers,

104
00:02:55,727 --> 00:02:57,784
and a caller can react to each.

105
00:02:59,834 --> 00:03:03,494
Now the part that makes exit codes powerful: the shell.

106
00:03:03,494 --> 00:03:05,624
Our closed message was an error,

107
00:03:05,624 --> 00:03:07,887
so it travelled on standard error.

108
00:03:07,887 --> 00:03:09,617
Throw standard error away,

109
00:03:09,617 --> 00:03:10,216
with two,

110
00:03:10,216 --> 00:03:11,082
greater than,

111
00:03:11,082 --> 00:03:11,747
slash dev,

112
00:03:11,747 --> 00:03:12,479
slash null.

113
00:03:12,479 --> 00:03:13,544
Type: dot slash,

114
00:03:13,544 --> 00:03:14,809
port check dot P Y,

115
00:03:14,809 --> 00:03:15,541
local host,

116
00:03:15,541 --> 00:03:15,807
one,

117
00:03:15,807 --> 00:03:16,206
space,

118
00:03:16,206 --> 00:03:16,472
two,

119
00:03:16,472 --> 00:03:17,338
greater than,

120
00:03:17,338 --> 00:03:18,003
slash dev,

121
00:03:18,003 --> 00:03:18,735
slash null.

122
00:03:19,285 --> 00:03:20,542
Nothing on screen.

123
00:03:20,542 --> 00:03:21,938
The message is gone,

124
00:03:21,938 --> 00:03:24,101
but the exit code is still one.

125
00:03:24,101 --> 00:03:25,497
Now the dash Q flag,

126
00:03:25,497 --> 00:03:27,312
with the double ampersand,

127
00:03:27,312 --> 00:03:31,570
which means: run the next command only if this one succeeded.

128
00:03:31,570 --> 00:03:32,687
Type: dot slash,

129
00:03:32,687 --> 00:03:34,013
port check dot P Y,

130
00:03:34,013 --> 00:03:34,501
dash Q,

131
00:03:34,501 --> 00:03:35,269
local host,

132
00:03:35,269 --> 00:03:36,525
eighty eighty-one,

133
00:03:36,525 --> 00:03:36,944
space,

134
00:03:36,944 --> 00:03:37,642
ampersand,

135
00:03:37,642 --> 00:03:38,340
ampersand,

136
00:03:38,340 --> 00:03:38,759
space,

137
00:03:38,759 --> 00:03:39,317
echo up.

138
00:03:39,867 --> 00:03:40,097
Up.

139
00:03:40,097 --> 00:03:42,543
The shell read a zero and chose.

140
00:03:42,543 --> 00:03:43,843
Now the opposite.

141
00:03:43,843 --> 00:03:49,042
The double pipe means: run the next command only if this one failed.

142
00:03:49,042 --> 00:03:50,265
Type: dot slash,

143
00:03:50,265 --> 00:03:51,718
port check dot P Y,

144
00:03:51,718 --> 00:03:52,253
dash Q,

145
00:03:52,253 --> 00:03:53,094
local host,

146
00:03:53,094 --> 00:03:53,400
one,

147
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:53,858
space,

148
00:03:53,858 --> 00:03:54,240
pipe,

149
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:54,623
pipe,

150
00:03:54,623 --> 00:03:55,081
space,

151
00:03:55,081 --> 00:03:55,846
echo down.

152
00:03:56,396 --> 00:03:56,738
Down.

153
00:03:56,738 --> 00:03:58,241
The tool said nothing,

154
00:03:58,241 --> 00:04:00,018
and the shell still chose,

155
00:04:00,018 --> 00:04:01,726
from the exit code alone.

156
00:04:01,726 --> 00:04:03,229
This is how pipelines,

157
00:04:03,229 --> 00:04:06,578
cron jobs and CI systems decide whether to go on.

158
00:04:06,578 --> 00:04:08,628
They never read your messages.

159
00:04:08,628 --> 00:04:10,063
They read the number.

160
00:04:10,063 --> 00:04:13,548
A tool that always exits zero lies to its pipeline.

161
00:04:15,598 --> 00:04:16,683
Here is your task,

162
00:04:16,683 --> 00:04:18,191
with everything you need.

